CAT = "phcleInvestigation"; SEG = "INV"
# Crime Detection and Investigation, part 1: fundamentals and intelligence, specialized crime
# investigation with legal medicine, interview and interrogation, technical writing, cybercrime
# and environmental laws.
Q = [
("The three tools of the investigator, the three I's of investigation, are information, interrogation and:",
 "Instrumentation", ["Identification", "Inspection", "Intelligence"],
 "Information, interrogation and instrumentation are the classic tools. Instrumentation means applying the sciences and laboratory methods to detect crime."),
("The golden rule in crime scene investigation is to:",
 "Never move evidence until it is documented", ["Collect evidence before photographing", "Let the first officer clear the scene", "Search the suspect before the scene"],
 "No evidence should be touched, altered or moved until it has been identified, measured, sketched and photographed, so its original position can be proved in court."),
("A crime scene search in which the searcher moves in ever-widening circles from the center outward is the:",
 "Spiral method", ["Strip method", "Zone method", "Wheel method"],
 "The spiral method circles outward (or inward) from a point. The wheel method sends searchers outward along spokes; the strip method uses parallel lanes; the zone method divides the area into sectors."),
("The body of the crime, meaning proof that a crime was actually committed, is the:",
 "Corpus delicti", ["Modus operandi", "Res gestae", "Mens rea"],
 "Corpus delicti is the fact that a specific offense was committed. Modus operandi is the offender's method of operation, often used to link crimes."),
("Watching a person from a fixed place, such as a parked vehicle or a rented room, is:",
 "Stakeout", ["Tailing", "Roping", "Casing"],
 "A stakeout or plant is stationary surveillance. Tailing or shadowing follows the subject on foot or by car; roping is undercover work; casing is surveillance of a place."),
("Under R.A. 7438, custodial investigation includes the practice of:",
 "Issuing an \"invitation\" to a suspect in an offense", ["Recording a complaint in the police blotter", "Interviewing a cooperative witness at the scene", "Serving a subpoena issued by a prosecutor"],
 "Section 2 of R.A. 7438 says custodial investigation includes issuing an \"invitation\" to a person investigated in connection with an offense he is suspected to have committed, so the rights to counsel and to remain silent apply then too."),
("In the evaluation of intelligence, a source graded \"A\" is:",
 "Completely reliable", ["Usually reliable", "Fairly reliable", "Reliability cannot be judged"],
 "The standard scale grades sources A (completely reliable) to F (reliability cannot be judged), and the information itself 1 (confirmed) to 6 (truth cannot be judged)."),
("The stiffening of the body's muscles after death, which usually begins within a few hours and later passes off, is:",
 "Rigor mortis", ["Livor mortis", "Algor mortis", "Cadaveric spasm"],
 "Rigor mortis is postmortem muscle stiffening. Livor mortis is the settling of blood, algor mortis the cooling of the body, and cadaveric spasm an instant stiffening at the moment of death."),
("A wound produced by a sharp-edged instrument, longer than it is deep, with clean edges, is:",
 "An incised wound", ["A lacerated wound", "A punctured wound", "A contusion"],
 "Incised wounds come from sharp edges such as knives or razors. Lacerated wounds from blunt force have irregular edges; punctured or stab wounds are deeper than they are long."),
("In hanging, the ligature mark on the neck is usually:",
 "Oblique, rising toward the knot", ["Horizontal and even all around", "Absent in every case", "Below the larynx at one level"],
 "The weight of the body pulls the noose upward, so the mark in hanging slants toward the knot. In ligature strangulation the mark is usually horizontal and of even depth."),
("Questioning a suspect who is expected to be reluctant, in order to obtain an admission or confession, is:",
 "Interrogation", ["Interview", "Inquest", "Deposition"],
 "Interrogation is the questioning of a suspect or a hostile witness. An interview is the friendly questioning of a cooperative witness or victim."),
("A technique in which one investigator acts hostile and a second acts sympathetic toward the suspect is the:",
 "Mutt and Jeff technique", ["Bluff on a split pair", "Stern approach", "Emotional appeal"],
 "In the Mutt and Jeff (good cop, bad cop) technique the friendly investigator offers relief from the hostile one. Bluff on a split pair tells one of two suspects that the other has talked."),
("Under R.A. 7438, an extrajudicial confession by a person under custodial investigation must be:",
 "In writing and signed in the presence of counsel", ["Made orally before the arresting officer and witnesses", "Recorded on video only", "Made within 24 hours of arrest"],
 "R.A. 7438 requires any extrajudicial confession to be in writing and signed in the presence of counsel, or in counsel's absence upon a valid waiver and in the presence of specified relatives or officials. Otherwise it is inadmissible."),
("A written statement of facts made under oath before a person authorized to administer oaths is:",
 "An affidavit", ["A blotter entry", "A spot report", "A memorandum"],
 "An affidavit is a sworn written statement, ending in a jurat by the officer who administered the oath. A blotter is the police logbook of incidents."),
("The police logbook in which incidents and complaints are recorded as they are reported is the:",
 "Police blotter", ["Spot report", "Progress report", "After-operations report"],
 "The blotter is the daily chronological record of incidents reported at a station. A spot report is the first quick report sent up the chain on a significant incident."),
("A short, immediate report on a significant incident sent to higher headquarters soon after it happens is a:",
 "Spot report", ["Final report", "Progress report", "Investigation report"],
 "A spot report gives the basic facts quickly, usually within hours. Progress reports follow developments, and the final report closes the case."),
("Under R.A. 10175, accessing the whole or any part of a computer system without right is:",
 "Illegal access", ["Data interference", "System interference", "Computer-related forgery"],
 "Illegal access is the first offense against the confidentiality, integrity and availability of computer data and systems in Section 4(a)(1). Data interference alters or deletes data; system interference hinders a system's functioning."),
("A court warrant that allows law enforcement to search a computer system and seize computer data is a:",
 "Warrant to Search, Seize and Examine Computer Data", ["Warrant to Disclose Computer Data", "Warrant to Intercept Computer Data", "Preservation order"],
 "The Rule on Cybercrime Warrants (A.M. No. 17-11-03-SC) provides for a WSSECD to search and seize. A WDCD compels disclosure of data, and a WICD allows interception."),
("The law that requires segregation of solid waste at source and bans open dumpsites is:",
 "R.A. 9003", ["R.A. 8749", "R.A. 9275", "R.A. 9147"],
 "R.A. 9003, the Ecological Solid Waste Management Act of 2000, mandates segregation and recovery. R.A. 8749 is the Clean Air Act, R.A. 9275 the Clean Water Act and R.A. 9147 the Wildlife Act."),
("Collecting, trading or killing a threatened wild animal without a permit violates:",
 "R.A. 9147", ["R.A. 9003", "R.A. 8550", "P.D. 705"],
 "R.A. 9147, the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act of 2001, punishes such acts. R.A. 8550 is the Fisheries Code and P.D. 705 the Revised Forestry Code."),
]
